19. Keep the bot running on the VPS
A Python process started inside a PowerShell window runs only while that process remains alive. For a first deployment, start the MT5 terminal manually, sign in, then start the bot manually and monitor it.

After the manual setup is stable, Windows Task Scheduler can launch the bot when the dedicated Windows user signs in.
19.1 Create a launcher file
In C:\Bots\EXPERT_ADVISOR_BOT, create run_bot.bat with:
@echo off
cd /d C:\Bots\EXPERT_ADVISOR_BOT
if not exist logs mkdir logs
.venv\Scripts\python.exe -u main.py >> logs\bot.log 2>&1
This writes standard output and errors to logs\bot.log. Make sure logs/ is ignored by Git if it contains private runtime information.
19.2 Create a scheduled task
1. Open Start → Task Scheduler.
2. Choose Create Task (not the basic task wizard, so you can review all settings).
3. On General, name it EXPERT_ADVISOR_BOT.
4. For the initial GUI-dependent setup, select Run only when user is logged on. Use the same Windows account that opens and connects the MT5 terminal.
5. On Triggers, create At log on for that Windows user. Add a short delay (for example, one minute) only after testing MT5 startup order.
6. On Actions, choose Start a program.
7. Program/script:
C:\Bots\EXPERT_ADVISOR_BOT\run_bot.bat
8. Start in (optional but recommended):
C:\Bots\EXPERT_ADVISOR_BOT
9. On Settings, configure a restart after failure only after testing the task. Prevent multiple simultaneous instances (choose the option that does not start a new instance if one is already running).
10. Save the task and test it manually from Task Scheduler.
11. Verify the MT5 terminal is connected before the bot task starts. A delayed task is not a substitute for checking the terminal and broker connection.
Important desktop-session limitation: MT5 is a desktop terminal. Do not assume that a task configured to run while no user is logged on will behave the same as a manually opened desktop terminal. Validate start-up, disconnect/reconnect, Windows restart, MT5 reconnection and bot logs on the actual VPS before depending on unattended operation. Disconnecting RDP normally leaves the Windows user session running; signing out closes it.
19.3 Restart and stop procedure
Before updating or restarting:
1. Stop the bot process.
2. Check the MT5 terminal and open DEMO positions.
3. Apply code updates.
4. Re-run dependency/import checks if packages changed.
5. Re-run the MT5 connection test.
6. Start only one bot process.
7. Check logs\bot.log and MT5 status.
Do not terminate an EC2 instance while relying on the bot to manage positions. If the VPS is stopped, terminated, disconnected or the Python process dies, the bot cannot continue running its management loop. Open broker-side positions may remain open; the trader must understand and manage that risk independently.
